cast: tomy

== campus | CAMPUS · LUNCH BREAK | sunny university campus courtyard with trees, benches and students' bags | bench
tomy (cheer): Quddus! I got admission with a tuition waiver! So it's fully funded, right?
quddus (think): Not exactly. A tuition waiver means you don't pay school fees. But who pays for your rent and food?
tomy (shock): Wait. Me?
quddus (talk): Yes, you. So let me explain the four words people mix up.
> 1f4da | FUNDING WORDS | 1f3eb Tuition waiver: no school fees | 1f4b5 Stipend: monthly money to live | 1f4b0 Fully funded: fees AND living costs | 1f4bc Assistantship: you work, they pay

== cafe | LATER · COFFEE SHOP | cozy coffee shop with warm lights, brick wall and plants | cafe
tomy (think): So fully funded means fees plus money to live?
quddus (thumbs): Usually yes. Many also add flights, visa costs, or health insurance. But always read the funding section.
tomy (worried): And assistantship? Is that like being a house help for the professor?
quddus (talk): No! It means you teach or do research part time, and in return they pay your fees or a salary. It's common for PhDs.
tomy (think): So before I celebrate, I should check: fees, living money, and what else they cover.
quddus (point): Exactly. And if it's only a tuition waiver, plan how you'll pay for living costs before you accept.
tomy (cheer): Okay. Celebration postponed. Research activated!
